WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:05.730
We use maps every day to figure out how
to walk from home to school, and then

2
00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:10.100
from school to soccer practice.
Scientists also use maps to navigate

3
00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:15.070
through the enormous amount of DNA in a
cell-- 3 billion base pairs in humans.

4
00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:20.760
The DNA is organized into several long
strands called chromosomes. Within each

5
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:25.320
chromosome are genes, regions of the
DNA that specify traits, like eye color

6
00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.270
or blood type. But how are these traits
ordered in the DNA, which genes are

7
00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:35.550
close together and which are far apart?
To answer this question, you need a map.

8
00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:41.810
Back in the early 1900s, two scientists
named Alfred Sturtevant and Thomas Hunt

9
00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:47.360
Morgan, made the first map of genes in
the fruit fly, Drosophila. But before

10
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.610
we get into Morgan and Sturtevant's
research and its implications, let's

11
00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:56.130
quickly review what was known at the
time about how traits are inherited.

12
00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:01.580
Fifty years earlier, Gregor Mendel, a
monk who performed experiments on pea

13
00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:06.170
plants, noted that traits, like whether
the plant's flowers were white or

14
00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:10.420
purple in color, or whether the peas
were round or wrinkled in shape,

15
00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:16.140
assorted independently. If you breed a
white flower, round pea plant with a

16
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:21.050
purple flower, wrinkled pea plant, you
can get any combination of these traits

17
00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.520
in the offspring plants.

18
00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.780
Thomas Hunt Morgan studied two traits
in Drosophila: eye color, which could

19
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:34.320
be either red or white, and the length
of the wings, long or short. If you

20
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.310
bred a white eyed, long winged fly with
a red eyed, short winged fly, the

21
00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:43.930
offspring almost always resembled one
of the parents. They were either white,

22
00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:48.250
long or red, short. Unlike the
experiments from Mendel, these traits

23
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:52.660
were not assorting independently.
Instead, these traits were, what he

24
00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:58.130
called, linked to each other. So what
was going on? Morgan guessed that the

25
00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:03.055
genes coding for these traits must lie
on the same chromosome. So when sperm

26
00:02:03.055 --> 00:02:08.035
or eggs are formed the two genes from
the same parent always went into the

27
00:02:08.035 --> 00:02:13.345
same sperm or egg cell, they stayed
linked together. In contrast, if they

28
00:02:13.345 --> 00:02:17.325
were on different chromosomes, then the
genes and their traits should sort

29
00:02:17.335 --> 00:02:19.535
independently, as described by Mendel.

30
00:02:20.765 --> 00:02:24.945
Interestingly, however, Morgan also saw
that occasionally some of the offspring

31
00:02:24.945 --> 00:02:29.585
from the white-long to red-short
breeding had a different appearance

32
00:02:29.585 --> 00:02:34.925
from the parents. He would sometimes
get white-short or red-long offspring

33
00:02:34.925 --> 00:02:39.065
flies. How could this have occurred if
the genes are linked? Morgan's

34
00:02:39.065 --> 00:02:43.525
hypothesis, which we now know is
correct, was that maternal and paternal

35
00:02:43.525 --> 00:02:48.775
chromosome pairs can exchange pieces of
DNA with each other. This exchange is

36
00:02:48.775 --> 00:02:53.545
called crossing over or recombination.
If the crossover point occurs in

37
00:02:53.545 --> 00:02:58.445
between the gene for eye color and the
gene for wing length, new combinations

38
00:02:58.445 --> 00:03:02.875
of traits, like white-short or
red-long, could be found in the

39
00:03:02.875 --> 00:03:07.825
offspring. So what does this have to do
with maps? Morgan's student, named

40
00:03:07.835 --> 00:03:12.575
Alfred Sturtevant, reasoned that the
frequency of crossover could be used to

41
00:03:12.575 --> 00:03:18.095
infer the approximate distance between
genes on a chromosome. And if we know

42
00:03:18.095 --> 00:03:20.465
distance between genes, we can make a
map.

43
00:03:21.725 --> 00:03:26.125
How did he do this? Let's look at the
chromosome pair below. If the genes for

44
00:03:26.125 --> 00:03:30.235
eye color and wing length are very far
apart from each other on the chromosome,

45
00:03:30.605 --> 00:03:34.735
it is very likely that recombination
could occur at a spot in between the

46
00:03:34.735 --> 00:03:39.725
two genes. This means it would be
likely to get new combinations of

47
00:03:39.735 --> 00:03:44.775
offspring that were distinct from their
parents. In contrast, if the eye color

48
00:03:44.785 --> 00:03:48.905
and wing length genes were very close
to each other on the chromosome, it is

49
00:03:48.905 --> 00:03:52.655
much less likely that the chromosomes
would cross over in between the two

50
00:03:52.655 --> 00:03:57.805
genes. So in this case, we would not
expect to get many offspring that had a

51
00:03:57.815 --> 00:04:02.695
different appearance from the parents.
Then Sturtevant analyzed six different

52
00:04:02.695 --> 00:04:07.235
genes that he knew were all found on
the same chromosome. He determined the

53
00:04:07.245 --> 00:04:10.818
percentage of crossover events that
occurred by looking at specific

54
00:04:10.818 --> 00:04:14.558
features in the offspring when he bred
different types of flies.

55
00:04:15.368 --> 00:04:19.228
The higher the percentage of crossing
over, the farther apart the two genes

56
00:04:19.228 --> 00:04:23.028
were on the chromosome, and the
lower the percentage of crossing over,

57
00:04:23.038 --> 00:04:28.588
the closer the two genes were. By this
method Sturtevant deciphered the order

58
00:04:28.598 --> 00:04:33.308
of the six genes on the chromosome and
approximately how far apart each one

59
00:04:33.308 --> 00:04:38.018
was from the other. This amazing
finding established that chromosomes

60
00:04:38.028 --> 00:04:42.608
are linear and that the genes are
organized within each chromosome in

61
00:04:42.608 --> 00:04:43.768
defined positions.

62
00:04:45.258 --> 00:04:50.408
The Sturtevant-Morgan findings have had
long term implications. Scientists have

63
00:04:50.408 --> 00:04:54.748
used similar genetic mapping strategies
to pinpoint the location of, and then

64
00:04:54.748 --> 00:05:00.228
clone, a gene of interest. On a grand
scale, scientists constructed genetic

65
00:05:00.228 --> 00:05:04.498
maps of thousands of genes in
Drosophila and humans, and then took

66
00:05:04.498 --> 00:05:09.368
this concept one step further to
sequence the entire 3 billion base pair

67
00:05:09.368 --> 00:05:10.118
human genome.

68
00:05:11.198 --> 00:05:15.418
The genome sequence is the ultimate
high resolution map. Even now,

69
00:05:15.428 --> 00:05:19.988
scientists use genetic mapping to find
a connection between a genetic disease

70
00:05:20.208 --> 00:05:24.008
that runs in families and gene
mutations that might underlie the

71
00:05:24.008 --> 00:05:24.528
disease.

